              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Grid Transformations**\n\nOkay, I'm starting to get a better handle on the grid transformations. It seems like the core concept revolves around the preservation or alteration of '0' values. In the training data, some '0's are definitely preserved, but in train 2 specifically, it's clear that some 0s are replaced by distinct color values (1, 2, 3), while others remain as zeros. I'm focusing on the examples from train 2, to see how the system is replacing the 0's to reach the desired pattern.\n\n\n**Investigating Zero Connectivity**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into the properties of zero-valued connected components. It seems size is a key differentiator, and my focus has shifted to train 1's output. The output seems to relate to components of zero's! For instance, all the \"1\"s in the output appear to map directly to size-3 connected components of zeros in the input. I'm checking if all instances follow this pattern, to see if component size is the only important feature.\n\n\n**Examining Zero Component Sizes**\n\nI've been calculating the size of zero-valued 4-connected components in the training input. It seems like the output value isn't based only on component size, but the inverse of component size seems closer. Ones, twos and threes represent component sizes of 3, 2 and 1 respectively. Now, I will check the remaining values to see if the inverse relationship holds consistently.\n\n\n**Confirming Size-to-Color Mapping**\n\nI'm now consolidating my observations. It appears size-4 components consistently remain zero, while other sizes translate to the colored values. Component sizes of 1, 2, and 3 are mapped to 3, 2, and 1, respectively. I'm verifying that this pattern holds across both training datasets.\n\n\n",
